\section{broom output shape}{

The \code{broom::tidy()} and \code{broom::glance()} methods of every table class,
\code{spicy_categorical_table}, \code{spicy_categorical_svy_table},
\code{spicy_continuous_table}, \code{spicy_continuous_svy_table},
\code{spicy_continuous_lm_table}, \code{spicy_outcome_table},
\code{spicy_multiresponse_table}, and \code{spicy_regression_table}, and the
\code{output = "long"} frame of every table function, speak one
vocabulary: broom's name wherever broom defines one, and one
snake_case name per concept otherwise, the same in every function.\tabular{ll}{
   Concept \tab Column \cr
   the variable a row describes, its displayed label \tab \code{variable}, \code{label} \cr
   the level of a categorical variable, the group of \code{by} \tab \code{level}, \code{group} \cr
   the role of the row's \code{level} (or item): a level, the row of missing values, ... \tab \code{.row_role} (\code{"level"}, \code{"missing"}, \code{"summary"}, ...) \cr
   the role of \code{group}: a group, the column of missing \code{by} values, the margin \tab \code{.group_role} (\code{"group"}, \code{"missing"}, \code{"total"}) \cr
   the \code{Total} margin of the groups \tab \code{total} (logical) \cr
   a count, a percentage, a proportion between 0 and 1 \tab \code{n}, \code{pct}, \code{proportion}, named by their base where a table has two (\code{pct_cases}, \code{pct_responses}, \code{proportion_cases}, \code{proportion_responses}) \cr
   the estimate of a row, its standard error and interval \tab \code{estimate}, \code{std.error}, \code{conf.low}, \code{conf.high} \cr
   the test: its name and statistic \tab \code{test_type}, \code{statistic} \cr
   the degrees of freedom, which follow the statistic \tab \code{df} for a \emph{t} or a chi-squared (\code{Inf} for a \emph{z}); \code{df} and \code{df.residual} for an \emph{F}, the Rao-Scott \emph{F} and the \emph{F} of a linear model included \cr
   the p-value, and the raw one under \code{p_adjust} \tab \code{p.value}, \code{p_unadjusted} \cr
   the effect size: its measure, value and interval \tab \code{es_type}, \code{es_value}, \code{es_ci_lower}, \code{es_ci_upper} \cr
   the standardized mean difference \tab \code{smd_type}, \code{smd_value} \cr
   the observations of a variable or of a model, and their weighted count \tab \code{nobs}, \code{weighted_nobs} \cr
   the weighted count of a group (beside its \code{n}) \tab \code{weighted_n} \cr
   the counts of the groups of \code{by}, of the categories of a variable \tab \code{n_groups}, \code{n_levels} \cr
   the fit of a model \tab \code{r.squared}, \code{adj.r.squared} \cr
}


\code{variable} holds the name of the column a row describes and \code{label}
its displayed label. \code{outcome} names the response of a regression
model and the single outcome of \code{\link[=table_outcome]{table_outcome()}}. The proportions of
\code{tidy()} are between 0 and 1, and the \code{Total} rows stay, flagged by
\code{total}. Each role column describes the key column it follows,
whatever the orientation of the printed table: \code{.row_role} the row's
\code{level}, \code{.group_role} its \code{group}. A level or a group added for
missing values is told apart by its role, \code{"missing"}, never by its
label, which follows the display language. In a table that compares
groups, the long output carries the effect-size and SMD columns
whether or not they were requested (\code{NA} then), and \code{glance()} has a
fixed set of columns; \code{p_value = FALSE} removes the test columns.
Model-level statistics (\code{nobs}, \code{r.squared}, ...) are in \code{glance()},
and in the long output only for \code{\link[=table_regression_uv]{table_regression_uv()}}, whose rows
are separate models. The printed objects carry the \code{by} column and its
label in the attributes \code{group_var} and \code{group_label}.

\code{output = "data.frame"} and \code{as.data.frame()} return the table as it
is displayed, one row per printed row, with numbers: the key columns
\code{variable}, \code{label} (and the \code{level} or \code{group} of the row, and
\code{.row_role}, the role of the printed row, where the table adds rows),
then the printed columns under their names. The two are identical for
every class. \code{output = "excel"} and \code{"word"} return the path of the
file, invisibly.

The set of columns produced by each method is considered
\strong{stabilizing} (\strong{experimental} for \code{\link[=table_multiresponse]{table_multiresponse()}}, as the
function itself): existing columns will not be
silently renamed or have their semantics changed within \verb{0.y.z},
and any breaking change is announced in \code{NEWS.md}. Adding optional new
columns (e.g. covariate-adjustment metadata) is not a breaking
change. Numeric columns keep the types downstream
broom-consumers expect: test degrees of freedom that are integer
by construction (chi-squared, factor-comparison F tests) stay
integer, while every degrees-of-freedom column that can be
fractional -- \code{df.residual}, the regression method's
per-coefficient \code{df}, and Welch-corrected test \code{df} -- is
numeric double, so Satterthwaite-corrected degrees of freedom
from cluster-robust variance modes are preserved verbatim
(matching \code{lmerTest::glance()} and the \code{afex} output
convention).
}

\section{Classed conditions}{

All errors and warnings emitted by the stable / stabilizing
surfaces carry classed conditions so downstream code can
dispatch on class via \code{tryCatch()} / \code{withCallingHandlers()}
instead of matching message strings. Each condition has a
package-wide parent class plus a leaf class describing the
specific cause:

\describe{
\item{\code{spicy_error}}{Catch-all parent for every error
raised by spicy. Leaves:
\itemize{
\item \code{spicy_invalid_input} -- bad argument value or type.
\item \code{spicy_invalid_data} -- bad data shape or content
(not a data.frame, length mismatch, \code{bit64::integer64}
columns, degenerate grouping).
\item \code{spicy_wrong_regime} -- signaled together with
\code{spicy_invalid_data} when a table receives the wrong kind
of data for its regime: a survey design handed to a plain table,
or a data frame handed to a survey twin. The message names the
function to use instead.
\item \code{spicy_missing_pkg} -- a Suggests dependency
is required by the requested operation but not installed.
\item \code{spicy_missing_quarto} -- \code{\link[=code_book]{code_book()}} was asked
for a PDF, and Quarto 1.7 or later, which compiles it, was
not found.
\item \code{spicy_typst_failed} -- Typst could not compile the
PDF of \code{\link[=code_book]{code_book()}}; the condition carries its messages in
\code{stderr}.
\item \code{spicy_missing_column} -- a referenced column
is not in \code{data}; the message names it. Signaled together
with \code{spicy_invalid_input}.
\item \code{spicy_unsupported} -- the operation is not
applicable to this input (e.g., Phi requested on a
non-2x2 table).
\item \code{spicy_not_implemented} -- signaled together with
\code{spicy_unsupported} when the request is well formed but
spicy does not compute it (e.g., weighted group tests in
\code{\link[=table_continuous]{table_continuous()}}); the message names an alternative.
\item \code{spicy_empty_table} -- \code{\link[=table_regression]{table_regression()}} has no row
left to show for a model once the requested row blocks are
switched off.
\item \code{spicy_ame_satt_unsupported_formula} -- signaled
together with \code{spicy_unsupported} when AME
Satterthwaite degrees of freedom are unavailable for the
model's formula structure; normally caught internally and
surfaced as a \code{spicy_fallback} warning.
\item \code{spicy_unsupported_class} -- the model class has
no regression-frame method, so \code{\link[=table_regression]{table_regression()}}
cannot render it.
\item \code{spicy_unsupported_vcov} -- the requested \code{vcov}
mode is not available for this model class.
\item \code{spicy_unsupported_standardized} -- the requested
\code{standardized} mode is not available for this model class.
\item \code{spicy_invalid_frame} -- an object failed the
structural validation of the internal regression-frame
contract behind \code{\link[=table_regression]{table_regression()}}.
\item \code{spicy_structured_version} -- \code{\link[=as_structured]{as_structured()}} received
a table built under an older version of the typed contract;
rebuild the table with the current spicy.
\item \code{spicy_resampling_failed} -- bootstrap /
jackknife resampling produced too few valid replicates to
estimate the requested statistic.
\item \code{spicy_defunct} -- an argument, or a value of an
argument, removed or renamed in a pre-1.0 hard break (e.g.,
\code{v_digits}, \code{effect_size = "eta_sq"}); the message names the
replacement. Signaled together with \code{spicy_invalid_input}
so generic input handlers still catch it.
\item \code{spicy_bad_authors} -- the \code{authors} argument of
\code{\link[=code_book]{code_book()}} has none of its accepted shapes. Signaled
together with \code{spicy_invalid_input}.
\item \code{spicy_label_collision} -- a label a summary table
adds (the row or column of missing values, the margin, a base
row, the \code{Overall} row) would be displayed beside a level, a
value or a label of yours that reads the same. Signaled
together with \code{spicy_invalid_input}; the message gives
the two ways out.
\item \code{spicy_internal} -- an internal precondition
failed; this is a bug in spicy, please report it.
\item \code{spicy_internal_invariant} -- an internal
consistency check on a spicy-built object failed and the
result cannot be trusted (see the warning leaf of the
same name for the renderable case).
}
}
\item{\code{spicy_warning}}{Catch-all parent for every warning.
Leaves:
\itemize{
\item \code{spicy_undefined_stat} -- the requested
statistic is undefined for this input; result is \code{NA}
(e.g., Tau-b on a table with all-zero marginals).
\item \code{spicy_negative_weights_no_test} -- signaled
together with \code{spicy_undefined_stat} when
\code{\link[=table_continuous_svy]{table_continuous_svy()}} or \code{\link[=table_categorical_svy]{table_categorical_svy()}}
withholds a group comparison because the analytic sample
carries negatively weighted rows; the estimates are still
reported and the table note says so.
\item \code{spicy_dropped_na} -- \code{NA} observations were
silently excluded from the computation (e.g., \code{NA}
weights).
\item \code{spicy_ignored_arg} -- an argument the call typed
has no effect in its context (e.g., \code{correct = TRUE} on a
non-2x2 table, \code{excel_path} without \code{output = "excel"}); the
warning names that argument and no other.
\item \code{spicy_no_selection} -- a selection or a filter
matched nothing, and an empty result is returned rather than
an error (the row-wise summaries, \code{\link[=varlist]{varlist()}}, a \code{levels_keep}
of \code{\link[=table_categorical]{table_categorical()}} that matches no level of a variable,
which is left out). In the table functions an empty column
selection is an error (\code{spicy_invalid_input}).
\item \code{spicy_no_match} -- a \code{keep} or \code{drop} pattern of
\code{\link[=table_regression]{table_regression()}} matched no coefficient term.
\item \code{spicy_fallback} -- the requested computation
failed; a simpler estimator was used instead.
\item \code{spicy_caveat} -- the computation succeeded but
its interpretation carries a non-trivial methodological
caveat (e.g., standardized coefficients on non-additive
terms).
\item \code{spicy_bayes_diagnostics} -- signaled together
with \code{spicy_caveat} when a Bayesian fit's sampler or
predictive-accuracy diagnostics miss their targets
(R-hat, ESS, divergences, E-BFMI, Pareto k, p_waic).
\item \code{spicy_nonconvergence} -- signaled together with
\code{spicy_caveat} when the fitting engine reports that
its optimizer did not converge. The table still shows the
numbers the model object holds, and a footer note says
what they are worth.
\item \code{spicy_model_choice} -- a defaulted modeling
choice was made on the user's behalf and is disclosed
(e.g., the linear probability model
\code{\link[=table_regression_uv]{table_regression_uv()}} fits to a binary outcome under
the default \code{method}).
\item \code{spicy_passthrough} -- a third-party warning
captured during an operation (e.g., the clipboard copy)
and re-emitted under the spicy taxonomy.
\item \code{spicy_typst_warning} -- Typst warned while
compiling the PDF of \code{\link[=code_book]{code_book()}}, which it still wrote;
the condition carries its messages in \code{stderr}. Signaled
together with \code{spicy_passthrough}.
\item \code{spicy_summary_failed} -- \code{\link[=varlist]{varlist()}} could not
summarize one column; the rest of the table is fine.
\item \code{spicy_renamed_column} -- a user data column or
factor level collided with a spicy-internal name and was
auto-renamed to preserve the data (emitted by
\code{\link[=cross_tab]{cross_tab()}}).
\item \code{spicy_internal_invariant} -- an internal
consistency check on a spicy-built object failed but the
output still renders, so the user sees both the table and
the diagnostic.
}
}
\item{\code{spicy_info}}{Parent for informational messages
(emitted via \code{rlang::inform()}; muffle with
\code{withCallingHandlers(spicy_info = ...)}). Leaf:
\code{spicy_silent_reference} -- reference levels are
displayed nowhere under \code{reference_style = "none"} with
\code{factor_layout = "flat"}. The once-per-session hint on
ordered-factor polynomial contrasts carries its own class,
\code{spicy_polynomial_contrasts_info}.
}
}
